"relationship": "same_topic",
"comparison": "Both BAC and GS define consolidation criteria for Variable Interest Entities (VIEs). BAC consolidates VIEs (like whole-loan trusts) if it has the power to direct the most significant activities and holds securities/contractual arrangements that could be significant. GS consolidates VIEs if it has variable interests providing both the power to direct activities significantly impacting economic performance and the obligation to absorb losses or right to receive significant benefits. The criteria for consolidation (power + significant interest/loss exposure) are substantively the same, though GS explicitly defines the VIE framework while BAC applies it to specific trust types.",
